  <h1>Peaking Blocks for Strength</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Marcus Reeve (MR), Editor-in-Chief &amp; Strength Programming Lead. Updated May 2026.</em></p>

  <p>A peaking block is a 4–8 week training phase designed to maximize 1RM expression on a specific target date — a powerlifting meet, a personal testing day, or a milestone lift. The goal of a peak is not to build new fitness (that happens in earlier accumulation phases) but to express the strength that's already been built by reducing accumulated fatigue while maintaining the neural patterns and movement quality needed to lift maximally.</p>

  <h2>What a peaking block actually does</h2>
  <p>Understanding the mechanism behind peaking explains why it works and why common mistakes undermine it. Your displayed strength on any given day is the difference between your fitness (actual strength capacity) and your fatigue (accumulated fatigue masking that capacity). During a hard training block, you accumulate significant fatigue — your performance in training might plateau or even decline slightly even as you're getting stronger, because the fatigue masks the fitness gains.</p>
  <p>A peaking block strategically reduces fatigue by cutting volume while maintaining intensity. As fatigue dissipates, your true strength capacity becomes visible. The deload week at the end of a peak is when you actually "get" the strength you've been building for months — not because you're doing something special that week, but because the fatigue is finally clearing. This is why skipping the deload is counterproductive: you never realize the full benefit of the training block that preceded it.</p>

  <h2>Sample 6-week peaking block</h2>
  <p>The following is a standard 6-week peaking template for a main lift (squat, bench, or deadlift). All percentages are relative to training max (90% of estimated 1RM):</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Week 1:</strong> 75% &times; 5 reps, 4 sets. Moderate intensity entry point. Still plenty of volume to maintain fitness.</li>
    <li><strong>Week 2:</strong> 80% &times; 4 reps, 4 sets. Intensity rises; volume holds.</li>
    <li><strong>Week 3:</strong> 85% &times; 3 reps, 3 sets. Entering heavy territory. Three sets of three at 85% is a significant strength stimulus.</li>
    <li><strong>Week 4:</strong> 90% &times; 2 reps, 3 sets. Near-maximal singles and doubles. You should be feeling strong and the weights should be moving well at this point.</li>
    <li><strong>Week 5:</strong> 95% &times; 1 rep, 2–3 sets. Opener-range singles. These should feel heavy but not maximal — if 95% of training max feels like an absolute grinder, your training max may be too high or you're carrying more fatigue than expected.</li>
    <li><strong>Week 6 (deload and test):</strong> 60% &times; 3 reps, 3 sets on Monday and Wednesday. Rest Thursday and Friday. Test or compete on Saturday/Sunday.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Accessory work decreases throughout the peak. In week 1, you might have full accessory programming. By week 5, accessories should be minimal — only what's needed to maintain movement quality and address specific weaknesses, not a full secondary workout on top of heavy primary lift work.</p>

  <h2>4-week peaking block option</h2>
  <p>For lifters who don't have 6 weeks before their target date, or who respond better to shorter peaks, a compressed 4-week structure:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Week 1:</strong> 80% &times; 3 reps, 4 sets</li>
    <li><strong>Week 2:</strong> 87% &times; 2 reps, 3 sets</li>
    <li><strong>Week 3:</strong> 93% &times; 1 rep, 2–3 sets</li>
    <li><strong>Week 4:</strong> Deload and test</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The 4-week peak requires that you entered it with a good fitness base and manageable fatigue levels. If you're coming off a very high volume accumulation block, you may need the full 6 weeks (or even longer) to clear fatigue adequately before the test.</p>

  <h2>Key principles of effective peaking</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Intensity up, volume down — simultaneously.</strong> Both adjustments are necessary. Maintaining high volume while raising intensity just adds more fatigue. Reducing volume while also reducing intensity doesn't prepare the nervous system for maximal effort. The combination is what creates the peaking effect.</li>
    <li><strong>Protect the deload.</strong> The final week before a test or meet should be low volume, moderate intensity. Do not add extra sessions, add "just one more" heavy set, or try to squeeze in additional technique work. The work is done — the deload week is purely about recovery and expression.</li>
    <li><strong>One primary lift per peaking block.</strong> Peaking the squat, bench, and deadlift simultaneously across the same 6-week window is possible for competition purposes, but it requires careful programming to avoid overloading any single training day. For practice peaks and personal records, focus on one lift at a time for best results.</li>
    <li><strong>Maintain movement quality throughout.</strong> As weights get heavier, the temptation is to grind through technical breakdowns. Don't. A clean, technically sound near-maximal single transfers to a clean maximal attempt. A sloppy 95% teaches sloppy technique.</li>
    <li><strong>Sleep and recovery are the peak.</strong> By week 5, the training is largely done. The quality of your sleep, nutrition, and stress management in weeks 5–6 will matter more than any additional training volume. This is when lifestyle factors that don't normally make a difference start to matter.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Common peaking mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Testing mid-peak "to see where you are."</strong> A 1RM attempt in week 3 or 4 of a peak is not an assessment — it's a fatigue-generating event that compromises the peak itself. You're not rested enough to display your true maximum, and the attempt leaves you more fatigued for the remaining weeks. If you must test, do it at the very end of the deload, not in the middle of the peak.</li>
    <li><strong>Skipping the deload because you "feel strong."</strong> Feeling strong in week 5 is exactly the point — fatigue is clearing and fitness is emerging. The deload completes this process. Skipping it because you feel good is like stopping a cake 10 minutes before it's done because the oven is hot. Trust the process.</li>
    <li><strong>Trying to PR every lift simultaneously.</strong> Powerlifting meets require you to peak three lifts at once, which is a genuine challenge. But in training, attempting to simultaneously peak your squat, bench, and deadlift often means none of them peaks optimally. Pick one priority lift per training cycle; maintain the others.</li>
    <li><strong>Adding accessory work during the peak.</strong> The peaking block is not the time to add new exercises, increase accessory volume, or try training methods you haven't used before. The unfamiliar stimulus creates additional fatigue and soreness that interferes with the peak.</li>
    <li><strong>Selecting attempts based on ambition rather than training data.</strong> For a meet or a formal test, your opener should be a weight you've successfully completed in training within the past 3–4 weeks. Your second attempt should be your established training max or slightly above. Your third attempt is the PR. Don't open with a PR attempt — a missed opener at a meet eliminates you from the total.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Meet day specifics</h2>
  <p>If you're competing in a sanctioned powerlifting meet, the following guidelines apply beyond the peaking structure:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Opener:</strong> 92–94% of training max. A weight you could execute on any day with good technique. The purpose is to get on the board and feel the competition environment. Do not open heavy.</li>
    <li><strong>Second attempt:</strong> Training max or 2–3% above. This should be a hard-but-confident lift, not a maximal grinder. Bar speed on your second attempt tells you how to calibrate the third.</li>
    <li><strong>Third attempt:</strong> 2–5% above second, if second moved well. Larger jumps on a third attempt (more than 5%) have low success rates in competition data. Small, confident jumps produce more PRs.</li>
    <li><strong>Warm-up timing:</strong> Time your competition warm-ups to peak around 20–30 minutes before your flight begins, not before the entire session. Take a final warm-up single at 85–90% of your opener approximately 10–15 minutes before you expect to take your first attempt.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>For the underlying programming that precedes a peaking block, see <a href="/guides/percentage-based-programming">percentage-based programming</a>. For testing a true 1RM outside of competition, see <a href="/guides/how-to-test-1rm-safely">how to test your 1RM safely</a>.</p>
